"Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2603.07615v1)\nciteturn32186view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn32184view0\",\"lineno\":217}); Total lines: 795\nL198: #### Inference-time scaling with sampling\nL199: \nL200: Inference-time scaling (cite120†Ma et al., 2025 ) for diffusion models uses extra computation at decode time to improve sample quality. A common strategy is to branch the SDE trajectory by drawing multiple candidates at each denoising step, then apply selection procedures such as sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) (cite121†Wu et al., 2023 ; cite122†Singhal et al., 2025 ; cite123†He et al., 2025 ) or search (cite124†Zhang et al., 2025 ) to retain the most promising candidates.\nL201: When we compress data via a functional representation, these inference-time scaling ideas carry over seamlessly: after the encoder obtains the LoRA adaption, they can run the generation process using a pseudo-random number generator (PRNG) shared with the decoder. At every time step, the encoder samples multiple particles and then selects the most promising one. The selected particle is then encoded by its index using a small amount of side information.\nL202: The decoder can then deterministically reproduce the same chosen particle at every step using the adaption and the shared PRNG. Note that the scaling happens mostly on the encoding side, while decoding remains relatively fast; hence this can be viewed as encoding-time scaling.\nL203: Let us now make the algorithm more concrete to our setting. First, we now assume both the encoder and the decoder access to the vector field $v_{\\theta}$, parametrized by the pretrained generative model, plus our compressed adaption. Then, in order to branch the denoising trajectory with multiple particles, we adopt the SDE formulation in cite114†Equation 3 . We integrate the SDE numerically by Euler-Maruyama method.\nL204: Concretely, we discretize the entire time horizon in $N$ steps, with boundaries $t_{0}=0<t_{1}<\\cdots<t_{N}=1$ and step size $\\Delta t=|t_{n}-t_{n-1}|$. Each denoising step is given by a Gaussian transition kernel:\nL205:  |  | $\\displaystyle p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})=\\mathcal{N}(x_{t_{n-1}}|\\mu_{\\theta}(x_{t_{n}}),\\frac{2t_{n}}{1-t_{n}}\\Delta t)$  |  | (6)\nL206:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mu_{\\theta}(x_{t_{n}})=x_{t_{n}}-\\left(\\frac{x_{t_{n}}}{1-t_{n}}+2v_{\\theta}(x_{t_{n}},t_{n})\\right)\\Delta t$  |  | (7)\nL207: As this distribution is available to both the encoder and the decoder, the encoder can use the shared PRNG to draw multiple samples and select the best candidate. The remaining question is: what criteria should the encoder use to determine the best sample?\nL208: \nL209: To answer this, let’s recall the training objective in cite113†Equation 1 . Its minimizer actually enjoys an analytical form:\nL210: \nL211:  | $\\displaystyle v^{*}(x_{t},t)=\\epsilon-x{\\mathrel{\\to}^{x_{t}=(1-t)x+t\\epsilon}}{(x_{t}-x)}/{t}$  |  | (8)\nL212: For any input $x_{t}$ and $t$, the encoder can access this form, as it is then just a linear function of $x$. This means that the encoder actually access to the optimal denoising kernel:\nL213: \nL214:  |  | $\\displaystyle p^{*}(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})=\\mathcal{N}(x_{t_{n-1}}|\\mu^{*}(x_{t_{n}}),\\frac{2t_{n}}{1-t_{n}}\\Delta t)$  |  | (9)\nL215:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mu^{*}(x_{t_{n}})=x_{t_{n}}-\\left(\\frac{x_{t_{n}}}{1-t_{n}}+\\frac{2(x_{t_{n}}-x)}{t_{n}}\\right)\\Delta t$  |  | (10)\nL216: Therefore, the encoder can perform importance sampling, with $p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})$ as the proposal and $p^{*}(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})$ as the target, to select the best particle. Precisely, we draw $M$ samples $x_{t_{n-1}}^{(1:M)}$ from $p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})$, and then select an index from a categorical distribution over ${1,\\dots,M}$, where each probability is proportional to the importance weight\nL217:  | $\\displaystyle w^{(m)}\\propto\\frac{p^{*}(x_{t_{n-1}}^{(m)}|x_{t_{n}})}{p(x_{t_{n-1}}^{(m)}|x_{t_{n}})}.$  |  | (11)\nL218: This ensures the selected one follows $p^{*}(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})$ when $M\\rightarrow\\infty$. The encoder repeats this sequentially for $n=N,N-1,\\dots,1$, until the final time step. We summarize the procedure in cite125†Algorithm 1 . This algorithm resembles Sequential Monte Carlo (cite126†Del Moral et al., 2006 , SMC, ), while only a single particle rather than the full particle population is retained at each step.\nL219: \nL220: cite127†Image: Refer to caption (a)\nL221: \nL222: cite128†Image: Refer to caption (b)\nL223: cite129†Image: Refer to caption (c)\nL224: \nL225: Figure 3: Reconstruction quality v.s. training step. (a) Common LoRA representations with different ranks for image Kodim03 from Kodak dataset (cite130†Kodak, 1993 ). (b) One-vector representations for image Kodim03, varying LoRA rank and vector size after hashing. (c) One-vector representations for video Beauty from UVG dataset (cite88†Mercat et al., 2020 ).\nL226: Notably, this procedure can also be viewed as a variant of the Diff-C algorithm (cite131†Theis et al., 2022 ), where the importance sampling with a fixed shared seed is a realization of the relative entropy coding (cite116†Havasi et al., 2019 ; cite132†Theis & Ahmed, 2022 ).\nL227: Therefore, we can interpret our inference-time scaling algorithm from two complementary perspectives: (1) from the inference-time refinement perspective, we perform importance sampling to refine the learned generative dynamics induced by $v_{\\theta}$; and (2) from the Diff-C perspective, we use the diffusion with adaptation as a stronger prior, thereby reducing the complexity of relative entropy coding.\nL228: We include more background on relative entropy coding and discussion on the connections in Appendix cite52†C.4 -cite54†C.6 .\nL229: #### Broader applicability: Functional representation via adaption for generation\nL230: The functional representations in our framework are stored directly as parameter adaptations attached to a generative model, allowing visual information to persist for new generation. The adaptations are learned jointly with captions that serve as semantic anchors, linking specific visual entities to adaptations. Even when the captions are changed, the model still remembers the entities via the adaptations.\nL231: In this sense, it can be viewed as persistent visual memories, consistent with the LoRA-based customization (cite81†Ruiz et al., 2023 ; cite82†Kumari et al., 2023 ; cite133†Zhang et al., 2023 ; cite134†Ren et al., 2024 ; cite135†Meral et al., 2025 ), potentially enabling editing and composition via text prompts. It supports both faithful reconstruction and potentially flexible editing, bridging compression and generative modelling.\nL232: ## 4 Experiments\nL233: ### 4.1 Image and Video Representation\nL234: We first evaluate the representation capability of the proposed implicit visual representations. Following the standard LoRA setup (cite80†Hu et al., 2022 ), we fine-tune all the transformer blocks in the frozen generative model by adapting query, key, value, and output projection layers to fit a given visual signal. Both the LoRA rank and the number of fine-tuning iterations are adjusted for studying their impact on reconstruction quality. Detailed experimental settings are described in Appendix cite62†E.1 .\nL235: As shown in Figure cite136†3(a) , increasing the common LoRA rank consistently improves reconstruction quality, while even a minimal setting of $\\text{rank}=1$ is sufficient to achieve reasonable reconstructions. Second, reconstruction performance exhibits a clear upper bound, which we attribute to the inherent information loss introduced by the tokenizer of the underlying image or video generative model.\nL236: #### One-Vector Representation\nL237: We further demonstrate that a single vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times k}$, obtained by hashing the optimized LoRA parameters, can effectively encode the visual content of either a single image or an 81-frame 480p video. As illustrated in Fig. cite137†3(b) and Fig. cite138†3(c) , increasing the vector dimensionality $k$ consistently improves reconstruction quality. Interestingly, when the vector size $k$ is fixed, we observe that increasing the LoRA rank leads to degraded reconstruction performance.\nL238: This counter-intuitive behavior highlights a non-trivial interaction between LoRA capacity and the one-vector encoding process. We hypothesize that higher-rank LoRA adaptations introduce denser and more entangled parameter updates, which become difficult to preserve under a fixed-size hashing scheme.\nL239: ### 4.2 Perceptual Video Compression\nL240: After representing the visual signal into a single-vector adaptation, we can further quantize this vector and apply entropy coding, yielding an extremely low-bitrate visual compression framework. Under this setting, the compressed representation prioritizes to boost perceptual reconstruction quality when decoded through a powerful generative model. We refer to this compression framework as V ision (Video) in O ne V ector (VOV).\nL241: Here, we demonstrate that VOV achieves strong perceptual video compression performance compared with existing video codecs.\nL242: #### Settings\nL243: We compare VOV against representative neural video codecs, including both MSE-optimized (cite139†Jia et al., 2025 ) and perceptually optimized methods (cite140†Qi et al., 2025 ). For reference, we also report statistics from traditional video codecs, including H.265/HM (cite141†HM, 2021 ) and H.266/VTM (cite142†VTM, 2021 ). Benchmark datasets include the UVG (cite88†Mercat et al., 2020 ) and HEVC B/C/E datasets (cite87†Flynn et al., ). We adopt Wan-2.1 (1.3B) (cite106†Wan et al., 2025 ) as the base video generative model.\nL244: Due to the default generation constraints of the model, videos are processed at a resolution of $832\\times 480$ with 81 frames. For fair comparison, all benchmark videos are center-cropped and resized to $480$p, and evaluated using 81-frame clips across all methods. Training details for compression are described in Appendix cite63†E.2 .\nL245: #### Metrics\nL246: We report DISTS (cite143†Ding et al., 2020 ), FVD (cite144†Unterthiner et al., 2018 ), Pixel-Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) as primary evaluation metrics. We note that conventional pair-wise reference-based metrics such as PSNR are not well suited for the extremely low-bitrate regime considered here, as they fail to reflect perceptual quality when reconstructions are generated by stochastic generative processes. We also provide LPIPS (cite145†Zhang et al., 2018 ) results in Appendix cite65†F for completeness.\nL247: cite146†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Comparisons with existing video codecs on UVG. For DISTS and FVD, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better.\nL248: \nL249: cite147†Image: Refer to caption Ground Truth\nL250: \nL251: cite148†Image: Refer to caption VTM\nL252: 0.0145 bpp\nL253: \nL254: cite149†Image: Refer to caption DCVC-RT\nL255: 0.0123 bpp\nL256: \nL257: cite150†Image: Refer to caption GLC-Video\nL258: 0.0120 bpp\nL259: \nL260: cite151†Image: Refer to caption VOV\nL261: 0.0109 bpp\nL262: \nL263: cite152†Image: Refer to caption VOV + Scaling\nL264: 0.0112 bpp\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2603.07615v1)\nciteturn32186view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn32184view0\",\"lineno\":685}); Total lines: 795\nL605: #### (2) Error introduced by error in the vector field.\nL606: \nL607: Now, let’s look at how $\\tilde{x}_{\\tau}$ is generated. We start from $x_{1}$ and follow the ODE backwards in time:\nL608: \nL609:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\tilde{x}_{t}}{\\mathrm{d}t}=v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)$  |  | (46)\nL610: \nL611: and recall the ground truth path is also known:\nL612: \nL613:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}{x}_{t}}{\\mathrm{d}t}=v^{*}({x}_{t},t)=x_{1}-x$  |  | (47)\nL614: \nL615: We can hence calculate the error\nL616:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\delta_{t}}{\\mathrm{d}t}=v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x),\\quad\\delta_{1}=0$  |  | (48)\nL617: \nL618: and therefore,\nL619: \nL620:  | $\\displaystyle\\delta_{t}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\int_{1}^{\\tau}(v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\\mathrm{d}t$  |  | (49)\nL621:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-\\int_{\\tau}^{1}(v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\\mathrm{d}t$  |  | (50)\nL622: #### (3) Putting them together.\nL623: \nL624: Putting the errors together, we obtain\nL625: \nL626:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\delta\\|=\\|\\int_{\\tau}^{1}(v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\\mathrm{d}t+\\tau(v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{\\tau},\\tau)-(x_{1}-x))\\|$  |  | (51)\nL627: \nL628: Now, introduce the error of the learned vector field:\nL629: \nL630:  | $\\displaystyle e_{t}=v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)$  |  | (52)\nL631: \nL632: Note that the error is introduced on the clean path $x_{t}$ instead of $\\tilde{x}_{t}$. Therefore, we have\nL633:  | $\\displaystyle\\|v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\\|$  | $\\displaystyle=\\|v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)+v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)\\|$  |  | (53)\nL634:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\|v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\\|+\\|v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)\\|$  |  | (54)\nL635: \nL636: As we further assume the learned network is $L$-Lipschitz continuous, hence\nL637:  | $\\displaystyle\\|v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\\|$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\|v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\\|+L\\|\\tilde{x}_{t}-x_{t}\\|$  |  | (55)\nL638:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\|e_{t}\\|+L\\|\\delta_{t}\\|$  |  | (56)\nL639: \nL640: Therefore, we have\nL641: \nL642:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\delta_{\\tau}\\|\\leq\\int_{\\tau}^{1}\\|v_{\\theta}(\\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\\|\\mathrm{d}t\\leq\\int_{\\tau}^{1}(\\|e_{t}\\|+L\\|\\delta_{t}\\|)\\mathrm{d}t$  |  | (57)\nL643: \nL644: With Grönwall’s inequality (cite197†Gronwall, 1919 ), we have\nL645:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\delta_{\\tau}\\|\\leq\\exp(L(1-\\tau))\\int_{\\tau}^{1}\\|e_{t}\\|\\mathrm{d}t$  |  | (58)\nL646: \nL647: Therefore, we have\nL648: \nL649:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\delta\\|\\leq(\\tau L+1)\\|\\delta_{\\tau}\\|+\\tau\\|e_{\\tau}\\|\\leq(\\tau L+1)\\exp(L(1-\\tau))\\int_{\\tau}^{1}\\|e_{t}\\|\\mathrm{d}t+\\tau\\|e_{\\tau}\\|$  |  | (59)\nL650: \nL651: ∎\nL652: ## Appendix E Experimental Details\nL653: \nL654: For image experiments, we use Qwen-Image (cite107†Wu et al., 2025 ) as our base model; and for video experiments, we use Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B-Diffusers (cite106†Wan et al., 2025 ) as our base model. We now describe additional experimental settings below.\nL655: ### E.1 Representation Experiments\nL656: \nL657: The detailed settings for experiments of image and video representations are described in Table cite198†1 and cite199†2 .\nL658: Training\nL659: ---\nL660: Image  | Kodim03 from (cite130†Kodak, 1993 )\nL661: Resolution  | 768x512\nL662: Generative model  | Qwen-Image-20B (cite107†Wu et al., 2025 )\nL663: Transformer number  | 60\nL664: LoRA rank $r$  | 1/2/4\nL665: LoRA position  | QKVO\nL666: One-vector size $k$  | $2^{15}$ / $2^{16}$ / $2^{17}$ / $2^{18}$\nL667: Training iterations  | 1000\nL668: Optimizer  | AdamW, weight decay 1e-4\nL669: Learning rate  | 0.0015\nL670: Batch size  | 64\nL671: Inference\nL672: ---\nL673: Denoising steps  | 50\nL674: Classifier-free guidance  | Disabled\nL675: Table 1: Settings for LoRA- or one-vector image representations.\nL676: Training\nL677: ---\nL678: Video  | Beauty from (cite88†Mercat et al., 2020 )\nL679: Resolution  | Cropped and resized to 832x480x81\nL680: Generative model  | Wan-2.1-1.3B (cite106†Wan et al., 2025 )\nL681: Transformer number  | 30\nL682: LoRA rank $r$  | 1/2/4/8\nL683: LoRA position  | QKVO\nL684: One-vector size $k$  | $2^{15}$ / $2^{16}$ / $2^{17}$ / $2^{18}$\nL685: Training iterations  | 700\nL686: Optimizer  | AdamW, weight decay 1e-4\nL687: Learning rate  | 0.002\nL688: Batch size  | 32\nL689: Inference\nL690: ---\nL691: Denoising steps  | 50\nL692: Classifier-free guidance  | Disabled\nL693: Table 2: Settings for one-vector video representations.\nL694: ### E.2 Compression Experiments\nL695: To compress the one-vector representation into bitstream, we adopt a two-stage training strategy. At the first stage, we train the one-vector representations without entropy constraint. Note that hashing mapping is normalized similar to (cite83†Li et al., 2025 ). Then, we load the pretrained vector and initialize the parameters entropy model to optimize them together.\nL696: During that time, the training objective is aggregation of the diffusion loss and the rate loss, where a Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda$ controls the trade-off. We describe other hyperparameters for video compression task in Table cite200†3 .\nL697: Training\nL698: ---\nL699: Resolution  | Cropped and resized to 832x480x81\nL700: Generative model  | Wan-2.1-1.3B (cite106†Wan et al., 2025 )\nL701: Transformer number  | 30\nL702: LoRA rank $r$  | 1\nL703: LoRA position  | QKVO\nL704: One-vector size $k$  | $2^{17}=131072$\nL705: Rate loss weight $\\lambda$  | [1.5e-3, 3e-3, 6e-3]\nL706: Training iterations  | 1000 for stage-1, 1000 for stage-2\nL707: Optimizer  | AdamW, weight decay 1e-4\nL708: Learning rate  | 0.0015\nL709: Batch size  | 64\nL710: Inference\nL711: ---\nL712: Denoising steps  | 100\nL713: Inference-time Scaling\nL714: ---\nL715: Denoising steps  | 100/1000\nL716: Sample per step  | $2^{18}/2^{10}$\nL717: Table 3: Hyperparameters for compression and scaling experiments.\nL718: ### E.3 Editing Experiments\nL719: Figure cite157†7 in the main paper presents a collection of image editing experiments enabled by the proposed implicit visual representations. In the second row of the figure, the left-most image is generated by overfitting a LoRA-based representation to a single image (Kodim23 from the Kodak dataset (cite130†Kodak, 1993 )), followed by sampling with a modified textual prompt. The second image is produced using the same procedure but with a different source image (Kodim07).\nL720: The third image demonstrates visual merging: a single set of LoRA-based representations is jointly optimized over two source images (Kodim07 and Kodim01). During training, the source image within each mini-batch is randomly sampled from the image set, encouraging the learned representation to encode shared visual characteristics. The fourth image is obtained in the same manner but extends the collective representation to three source images. Finally, the right-most image illustrates resolution editing.\nL721: After learning an implicit visual representation at a resolution of $768\\times 512$, the generation resolution is changed to $512\\times 768$ at inference time. Since the underlying diffusion foundation model supports variable-resolution generation, this experiment shows that the proposed implicit visual representations naturally inherit this capability, enabling flexible resolution adaptation without retraining.\nL722: We emphasize that the editing experiment here is zero-shot, and the used base model originally does not accept native visual input.\nL723: ## Appendix F Additional Results\nL724: ### F.1 Full RD Curve\nL725: cite201†Image: Refer to caption Figure 13: Comparisons with existing video codecs on HEVC-B. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better. cite202†Image: Refer to caption Figure 14: Comparisons with existing video codecs on HEVC-C. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better. cite203†Image: Refer to caption Figure 15: Comparisons with existing video codecs on HEVC-E. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better.\nL726: cite204†Image: Refer to caption Figure 16: Comparisons with existing video codecs on UVG. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better.\nL727: ### F.2 Visualization of Compressed Videos\nL728: As shown in Figure cite205†19 , we provide more visualizations of decoded videos for qualitative comparison. The scaling results is with 1000 denoising steps, and $2^{10}$ samples per step. In addition, we save the decoded videos into mp4 files to supplementary material for better demonstration of the advantage of our method. It is shown that our method achieves much better perceptual quality, such as smooth temporal coherence, which cannot be reflected by rate-distortion curves.\nL729: cite206†Image: Refer to caption Ground Truth\nL730: \nL731: cite207†Image: Refer to caption VTM\nL732: 0.0211 bpp\nL733: \nL734: cite208†Image: Refer to caption DCVC-RT\nL735: 0.0149 bpp\nL736: \nL737: cite209†Image: Refer to caption GLC-Video\nL738: 0.0152 bpp\nL739: \nL740: cite210†Image: Refer to caption VOV\nL741: 0.0126 bpp\nL742: \nL743: cite211†Image: Refer to caption VOV + Scaling\nL744: 0.0129 bp\nL745: \nL746: cite212†Image: Refer to caption Ground Truth\nL747: \nL748: cite213†Image: Refer to caption VTM\nL749: 0.0179 bpp\nL750: \nL751: cite214†Image: Refer to caption DCVC-RT\nL752: 0.0157 bpp\nL753: \nL754: cite215†Image: Refer to caption GLC-Video\nL755: 0.0131 bpp\nL756: cite216†Image: Refer to caption VOV\nL757: 0.0127 bpp\nL758: \nL759: cite217†Image: Refer to caption VOV + Scaling\nL760: 0.0130 bpp\nL761: \nL762: cite218†Image: Refer to caption Ground Truth\nL763: \nL764: cite219†Image: Refer to caption VTM\nL765: 0.0093 bpp\nL766: \nL767: cite220†Image: Refer to caption DCVC-RT\nL768: 0.0127 bpp\nL769: \nL770: cite221†Image: Refer to caption GLC-Video\nL771: 0.0116 bpp\nL772: \nL773: cite222†Image: Refer to caption VOV\nL774: 0.0083 bpp\nL775: \nL776: cite223†Image: Refer to caption VOV + Scaling\nL777: 0.0086 bp\nL778: \nL779: Figure 19: Additional visual comparisons with different video codecs.\nL780: Experimental support, please cite224†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite225†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL781: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL782: \nL783: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL784: \nL785:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL786: \nL787: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL788: Our team has already identified cite226†the following issues†github.com . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL789: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a cite227†list of packages that need conversion†github.com , and welcome cite228†developer contributions†github.com .\nL790: \nL791: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite229†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL792: cite230†About†info.arxiv.org · cite231†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite232†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite233†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite234†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite235†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite236†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite237†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L793: \nL794: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompression as Adaptation: Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models - Microsoft Research (https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/compression-as-adaptation-implicit-visual-representation-with-diffusion-foundation-models/)\nciteturn32186view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn32169view3\",\"lineno\":12}); Total lines: 44\nL0: # Compression as Adaptation: Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models\nL1: \nL2:   * Jiajun He ,\nL3:   * Zongyu Guo ,\nL4:   * Zhaoyang Jia ,\nL5:   * cite0†Xiaoyi Zhang ,\nL6:   * Jiahao Li ,\nL7:   * Xiao Li ,\nL8:   * Bin Li ,\nL9:   * José Miguel Hernández-Lobato ,\nL10:   * cite1†Yan Lu L11: \nL12: 2026 ICML 2026 | March 2026\nL13: \nL14: cite2†Publication†arxiv.org L15: \nL16: cite3†Download BibTex L17: Modern visual generative models acquire rich visual knowledge through large-scale training, yet existing visual representations (such as pixels, latents, or tokens) remain external to the model and cannot directly exploit this knowledge for compact storage or reuse. In this work, we introduce a new visual representation framework that encodes a signal as a function, which is parametrized by low-rank adaptations attached to a frozen visual generative model.\nL18: Such implicit representations of visual signals, \\textit{e.g.}, an 81-frame video, can further be hashed into a single compact vector, achieving strong perceptual video compression at extremely low bitrates. Beyond basic compression, the functional nature of this representation enables inference-time scaling and control, allowing additional refinement on the compression performance.\nL19: More broadly, as the implicit representations directly act as a function of the generation process, this suggests a unified framework bridging visual compression and generation.\nL20: Opens in a new tab\nL21: \nL22: cite4†Publication†dblp.org L23: \nL24: Follow us:\nL25: \nL26:   * cite5†Follow on X†x.com L27:   * cite6†Like on Facebook†www.facebook.com L28:   * cite7†Follow on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L29:   * cite8†Subscribe on Youtube†www.youtube.com L30:   * cite9†Follow on Instagram†www.instagram.com L31:   * cite10†Subscribe to our RSS feed L32: \nL33: Share this page:\nL34: \nL35:   * cite11†Share on X†x.com L36:   * cite12†Share on Facebook†www.facebook.com L37:   * cite13†Share on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L38:   * cite14†Share on Reddit†www.reddit.com L39: cite15†Surface Pro cite16†Surface Laptop cite17†Surface Laptop Ultra cite18†Surface RTX Spark Dev Box cite19†Explore Copilot cite20†Explore Microsoft products cite21†Windows 11 apps cite22†Account profile†account.microsoft.com cite23†Download Center cite24†Microsoft Store support†go.microsoft.com cite25†Returns cite26†Order tracking cite27†Certified Refurbished cite28†Microsoft Store Promise cite29†Flexible Payments cite30†Microsoft in education cite31†Devices for education cite32†Microsoft Teams for Education cite33†Microsoft 365 Education cite34†How to buy for your school cite35†Educator training and development†education.microsoft.com cite36†Deals for students and parents cite37†AI for education L40: cite38†Microsoft AI cite39†Microsoft Security cite40†Dynamics 365 cite41†Microsoft 365 cite42†Microsoft Power Platform cite43†Microsoft Teams cite44†Microsoft Copilot cite45†Small Business cite46†Azure†azure.microsoft.com cite47†Microsoft Developer†developer.microsoft.com cite48†Microsoft Learn†learn.microsoft.com cite49†Support for AI marketplace apps cite50†Microsoft Tech Community†techcommunity.microsoft.com cite51†Microsoft Marketplace†marketplace.micro